course: dictionary
lesson: 4676
title: Holly: the plant or the name?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one. Holly is a plant: an evergreen bush with shiny, prickly, dark green leaves and bright red berries in winter. Its branches decorate homes and churches at Christmas.
explain: Look for clues around the word. In the carol «The Holly and the Ivy», the holly is a plant that sits next to the ivy, another plant. A capital H, as in «Holly's family moved from England to Australia», tells you it is a person's name.
explain: Ask yourself: what is this word pointing to here? Then swap in your meaning. If «a holly wreath on the door» still makes sense with the plant meaning, the plant is the answer.
fact: Holly is a noun, and its plural is hollies.
fact: Holly is an evergreen: it keeps its leaves in winter.
fact: Its leaves are shiny, prickly and dark green, and its berries are bright red in winter.
fact: A sprig of holly is a small branch, often used to decorate a home at Christmas.
fact: Holly with a capital H, as in «Holly's family moved to Australia», is usually a person's name.
exercise: «The bush had shiny, prickly leaves and bright red berries.» What is holly here? | A cold wind from the north | *An evergreen bush with red berries in winter | A kind of cake | A bird that sings at night
exercise: Mum hung a holly wreath on the front door at Christmas. What is holly used for here? | To feed the birds in summer | To mark the start of the school holidays | To make a fire | *To decorate the house
exercise: «Holly was tired after her long walk home.» Is Holly a plant or a name here? | *A person's name | A Christmas song | A plant | A type of bird
test: In winter, what colour are holly's berries? | Pure white | Blue | *Bright red | Yellow and black
test: In the carol «The Holly and the Ivy», what is holly linked to? | *Jesus | A football team | A summer picnic | A school bus
exam: Which phrase uses holly as a plant? | *A sprig of holly | Holly and Sam went out | Holly has a cold | Holly said hello
